#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151824 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152270 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d510b2f-df38-5fbe-a420-4ea79135488c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Constant of Proportionality"}] :question/id #uuid "e85107d6-ecdf-526d-a7ce-091d028039eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-13098" :question/problem "Adam started writing a book. There is a proportional relationship between the number of pages $x$ he writes and the book's thickness $y$ (in millimeters). Adam measured the thickness of his book to be $3$ millimeters after he'd written $18$ pages.\n\nWhat is the constant of proportionality for the relationship between $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "We are told that after Adam wrote $x=18$ pages, the book's thickness was $y=3$ millimeters.\n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-value to the $x$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x} = \\dfrac{3}{18} = \\dfrac{3 \\div 3}{18 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $\\dfrac{1}{6},$ and its units are \"millimeters per page.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151825 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152271 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a9d0449-2b61-5979-bd5c-94c33c8899c2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Equation of a Proportional Relationship"}] :question/id #uuid "15f20ecd-503f-5748-b004-680b5fc127c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-13099" :question/problem "Beatrice needs to clean the garage. So, to motivate herself, she allows herself $10$ minutes of video game time for every $25$ minutes she spends cleaning.\n\nThere is a proportional relationship between the time $x$ (in minutes) Beatrice spends cleaning the garage and the time $y$ (in minutes) she plays video games. What equation represents the relationship between $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Every proportional relationship takes the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nWe are told that for every $x=25$ minutes Beatrice spends cleaning, she will play $y=10$ minutes of video games.\n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-value to the $x$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x} = \\dfrac{10}{25} = \\dfrac{10 \\div 5}{25 \\div 5} = \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{2}{5} x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151826 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152272 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f29a835-91e3-51e3-a016-e13d296b7654" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Amount"}] :question/id #uuid "6b17572d-a8f7-523f-9697-ae16f3d84398" :question/math-academy-id "e-13100" :question/problem "Amy is baking a loaf of bread. If the loaf weighs $12$ ounces, then Amy needs to bake the loaf for $40$ minutes. There is a proportional relationship between the loaf's weight (in ounces) and the time (in minutes) the loaf needs to bake.\n\nIf Amy leaves the house for $60$ minutes and wants the loaf to be fully baked the moment she returns, how much should the loaf of bread weigh?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the loaf's weight (in ounces), and $y$ be the time (in minutes) the loaf needs to bake.\n\nFirst, we need to construct the equation that represents the proportional relationship. Every proportional relationship takes the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nWe are told that if the loaf weighs $x=12$ ounces, then Amy needs to bake it for $y=40$ minutes. We can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-value to the $x$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x} = \\dfrac{40}{12} = \\dfrac{40 \\div 4}{12 \\div 4} = \\dfrac{10}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{10}{3} x.\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find the value of $x$ so that the loaf will bake for exactly $y=60$ minutes. So, we substitute $y=60$ into the equation above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =\\frac{10}{3}x \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}x & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve for $x,$ we multiply both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{10}{3},$ which is $\\dfrac{3}{10}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{10}\\times \\frac{10}{3}x & =\\frac{3}{10}\\times 60 \\\\ 1x & =\\frac{3\\times 60}{10} \\\\ x & =\\frac{180}{10} \\\\ x & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the loaf of bread should weigh $18$ ounces."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152271 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a9d0449-2b61-5979-bd5c-94c33c8899c2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Equation of a Proportional Relationship"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14b24837-3232-5166-aee1-a19680d67c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f=-12g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497845} {:answer/id #uuid "3a280ebf-59cf-5d74-b77a-80449f4a1773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f=-9g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497931} {:answer/id #uuid "67b06279-e618-56ce-875d-a44035d091a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f=-7g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498047} {:answer/id #uuid "8f3e3a31-651c-553b-9956-db3dbf6920ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f=12g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498129} {:answer/id #uuid "f96bce90-f433-5f96-bda5-88d6982e9ff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f=7g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14b24837-3232-5166-aee1-a19680d67c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f=-12g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497845} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7a06b64-073c-57ea-a18a-32470f23b71e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9cbb3a3-7c34-5581-b4b4-9fd9c86b9859" :question/math-academy-id "q-115573" :question/problem "In a direct variation, $f=84$ when $g=-7.$ What is the relationship between $f$ and $g?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ varies directly with $g,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\nf=kg.\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that $f=84$ when $g=-7$ to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}84 & =k\\cdot (-7) \\\\ \\frac{84}{(-7)} & =k \\\\ -\\frac{84}{7} & =k \\\\ -12 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our direct variation equation is\n\n$$\nf=-12g.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152272 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f29a835-91e3-51e3-a016-e13d296b7654" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Amount"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "281ab47b-8c5b-5f64-bf9a-faea1728fdb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "281ab47b-8c5b-5f64-bf9a-faea1728fdb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497881} :answer-field/id #uuid "e95dfe78-b1e7-5a61-bce1-8aaf37b7c796" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3fee2173-5ead-559d-b784-c9f6f5226ecd" :question/math-academy-id "q-185468" :question/problem "Suppose $p$ is directly proportional to $q.$ Given that $p=-35$ when $q=-7$, find the value of $p$ when $q=-6.$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ varies directly with $q,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\np=kq.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $p=-35$ when $q=-7.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-35 & =k\\cdot (-7) \\\\ \\frac{(-35)}{(-7)} & =k \\\\ \\frac{35}{7} & =k \\\\ 5 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the direct variation equation is given by\n\n$$\np=5q.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the value of $q=-6$ into this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =5\\cdot (-6)=-30.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498525 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152270 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d510b2f-df38-5fbe-a420-4ea79135488c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Constant of Proportionality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "563787d7-2576-598b-b6c7-f9aa7f3e09c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ people per sandwich" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498002} {:answer/id #uuid "8883ae3f-e938-543c-8a67-194825c2778c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{2}{5}$ sandwiches per person" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498115} {:answer/id #uuid "a914ec22-a0e9-5f52-9f7e-1bcc144fcc2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{12}$ sandwiches per person" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498172} {:answer/id #uuid "db1f50f9-dc61-548e-8b7e-1947c396f01e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{2}$ sandwiches per person" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498289} {:answer/id #uuid "ee76e084-8869-58e1-8ba3-724bc9507869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$30$ people per sandwich" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db1f50f9-dc61-548e-8b7e-1947c396f01e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{2}$ sandwiches per person" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498289} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5f44313-2822-5c69-bc24-8d6493b66567" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41a80b0b-ddbc-5443-a94b-c85298f9a320" :question/math-academy-id "q-68390" :question/problem "Andry is planning a party. He made $30$ sandwiches for $12$ people at the last party. If there is a proportional relationship between the number of people at the party $x$ and the number of sandwiches $y,$ what is the constant of proportionality for the relationship between $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "We are told that Andry made $y=30$ sandwiches for $x=12$ people.\n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-value to the $x$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{30}{12}=\\frac{30÷6}{12÷6}=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $\\frac{5}{2},$ and its units are \"sandwiches per person.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152270 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d510b2f-df38-5fbe-a420-4ea79135488c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Constant of Proportionality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a1f0fad-5027-53d9-b9be-b6bde3559b6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$30$ miles per gallon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497861} {:answer/id #uuid "5487d8de-f95f-5485-a8d7-75d1c9af7508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$40$ miles per gallon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497998} {:answer/id #uuid "a877ee85-4fcd-5c4b-b41d-c0fab7724908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$48$ gallons per mile" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498171} {:answer/id #uuid "b4cc1c41-5eee-5f69-a7b1-3d50db1c54f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ miles per gallon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498202} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a745d5-460e-5486-bb13-3edfca2e9688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.3$ gallons per mile" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a1f0fad-5027-53d9-b9be-b6bde3559b6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$30$ miles per gallon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497861} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb2de326-5c77-59a4-8469-5f6eb55c27a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b9b1a1f6-9b02-546d-b293-0d13315885e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-68403" :question/problem "Caroline bought a new car. The company claims she can drive the vehicle for $120$ miles with $4$ gallons of gasoline. If there is a proportional relationship between the volume of gas $x$ (in gallons) and the distance traveled $y$ (in miles), what is the constant of proportionality for the relationship between $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "We are told that Caroline can drive the car for $y=120$ miles with $x=4$ gallons of gasoline.\n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-value to the $x$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{120}{4}=\\frac{120÷4}{4÷4}=\\frac{30}{1}=30\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $30$, and its units are \"miles per gallon.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152272 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f29a835-91e3-51e3-a016-e13d296b7654" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Amount"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61de65fc-1b91-5ef8-abed-e4196e46ccf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ cups" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498032} {:answer/id #uuid "a2b31188-bf02-5449-b4d4-64765c8eb00a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,\\frac{8}{9}$ cups" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498162} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b5c3ac-10d9-55fc-9fec-4725966b7cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{3}{8}$ cups" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498200} {:answer/id #uuid "cba1adc8-a381-58fd-919d-a2c32439a8bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ cups" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498255} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c24ae1-00d2-501c-8cd5-5ee3e226c7cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\frac{2}{3}$ cups" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4c24ae1-00d2-501c-8cd5-5ee3e226c7cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\frac{2}{3}$ cups" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498336} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ff7da26-9e1b-50c0-be73-b690001b1faf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "48f6d6fb-eebd-57a1-90a2-1f718d690dc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-68813" :question/problem "Jane is making lemonade for her friends. According to the recipe, $4$ cups of lemon juice are required for $18$ glasses of lemonade. There is a proportional relationship between the number of cups of lemon juice and the number of glasses of lemonade. If Jane needs to make $12$ glasses of lemonade, how many cups of lemon juice does she need?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the number of cups of lemon juice and $y$ be the number of glasses of lemonade.\n\nFirst, we need to construct the equation that represents the proportional relationship. Every proportional relationship takes the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nWe are told that $x=4$ cups of lemon juice are required for $y=18$ glasses of lemonade. We can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-value to the $x$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{18}{4}=\\frac{18÷2}{4÷2}=\\frac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{9}{2}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find the value of $x$ so that Jane can make $y=12$ glasses of lemonade. So, we substitute $y=12$ into the equation above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =\\frac{9}{2}x \\\\ \\frac{9}{2}x & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve for $x,$ we multiply both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of $\\frac{9}{2},$ which is $\\frac{2}{9}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9}\\times \\frac{9}{2}x & =\\frac{2}{9}\\times 12 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\times 12}{9} \\\\ x & =\\frac{24}{9} \\\\ x & =\\frac{24÷3}{9÷3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{3}=2\\,\\text{R}2=2\\,\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Jane requires $2\\,\\frac{2}{3}$ cups of lemon juice to make $12$ glasses of lemonade."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152270 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d510b2f-df38-5fbe-a420-4ea79135488c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Constant of Proportionality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f66ffbd-4ab3-5a0d-ad10-3c84b10f1be0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$7$ per gallon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497834} {:answer/id #uuid "26502048-b790-589f-990a-26fda1a87b6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$5$ per gallon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497876} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4a9087-c890-5988-bffa-616597615a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$4$ per gallon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497893} {:answer/id #uuid "35560f4c-d160-550c-926d-289ce82100e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3$ per gallon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497917} {:answer/id #uuid "f96e8ea3-4325-52a2-8778-723b8f56b8ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$9$ per gallon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35560f4c-d160-550c-926d-289ce82100e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3$ per gallon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497917} :answer-field/id #uuid "a92b222a-5f2f-5323-9bd3-8b69666aed7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48d16888-fa09-5ea2-9260-b0fddb4cefed" :question/math-academy-id "q-91985" :question/problem "Tom purchased $7$ gallons of gas for $$21$ dollars. If there is a proportional relationship between the cost of gas $y$ (in dollars) and the volume of gas $x$ (in gallons), what is the constant of proportionality for the relationship between $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "We are told that Tom paid $y=21$ dollars for $x=7$ gallons of gas.\n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-value to the $x$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{21}{7}=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $3$, and its units are \"dollars per gallon.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498489 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152271 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a9d0449-2b61-5979-bd5c-94c33c8899c2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Equation of a Proportional Relationship"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4596e399-780c-5a6e-9f4d-476649bce45f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=18x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497956} {:answer/id #uuid "512ce739-9246-50b3-81fd-ab7d40bd2452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497991} {:answer/id #uuid "ca292b8b-0498-5fb9-af05-7e1eec858a35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=24x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498248} {:answer/id #uuid "d636311a-db7e-58ce-82ad-e9ad17c6f690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=36x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498279} {:answer/id #uuid "dce40e19-adf9-53a0-a87d-2fe828922741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca292b8b-0498-5fb9-af05-7e1eec858a35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=24x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498248} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2bad046-a923-58f2-bdea-1c860f46c975" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "094ce64c-3d3e-57b3-9487-feae2ee86b9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-92002" :question/problem "William read $72$ pages of a best-selling novel in $3$ hours. There is a proportional relationship between time $x$ William spends reading (in hours) and the number of pages $y$ he reads. What is the equation that represents the relationship between $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Every proportional relationship takes the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nWe are told that William read $y=72$ pages of the novel in $x=3$ hours.\n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-value to the $x$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{72}{3}=24\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny=24x.\n$$"}]}
